    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm currently working on a site in the Peloponnese and I'm looking at a collection of highly fragmented ceramic shards from a single stratum. I'm having some trouble distinguishing between different firing techniques used in this period. Could you explain the key indicators to look for when trying to differentiate between oxidized and reduced firing atmospheres in terracotta fragments?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Oxidized firing typically results in warm, reddish-brown to yellow hues due to the presence of iron oxides reacting with oxygen. Conversely, reduced firing creates a more muted palette, often exhibiting dark grays, blacks, or even greenish tones as oxygen is restricted during the firing process. Look closely at the clay body color and any surface slips for these characteristic color changes. These variations in color are direct indicators of the atmospheric conditions experienced during the heating cycle.",
      "source": "target",
