    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. I'm also looking at some very small, irregular fragments that seem almost too scattered to reconstruct a full profile. Sometimes the way they're distributed in the soil layer feels almost deliberate or chaotic rather than just natural decay. Are there specific ways to analyze the grain structure or the cross-section of these tiny pieces to confirm if they were part of a high-temperature kiln firing?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "To confirm high-temperature firing, examine the microstructure for evidence of vitrification or quartz grain rounding, which occur as materials reach higher temperatures. If the clay matrix shows significant sintering or changes in mineralogy, it suggests intense heat exposure. Also, look for micro-cracks that indicate rapid cooling, rather than simple breakage from burial. These microscopic features provide definitive clues about the thermal history of the ceramics.",
      "source": "target",
